<pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.8" change_id="I6f358970e530069f82c8785a7ffd9e278b2e0f05" commit="2089684f9bbd07a507ad20c936ea89c69ad59a2a" commit_message="CHROMIUM: Remove dma-buf range-based flush for pl111 driver  Seems that dma-buf range-based flush are not used at all in pl111 and we want to pave that for the upcoming commits on dma-buf.  BUG=chromium:487189  Signed-off-by: Tiago Vignatti &lt;tiago.vignatti@intel.com&gt;  Change-Id: I6f358970e530069f82c8785a7ffd9e278b2e0f05 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/327472 Commit-Ready: Tiago Vignatti &lt;tiago.vignatti@intel.com&gt; Tested-by: Tiago Vignatti &lt;tiago.vignatti@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Stéphane Marchesin &lt;marcheu@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="327472" owner_email="tiago.vignatti@intel.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/72/327472/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.8" change_id="I84d5b0c1f41d098bd3c64c7f7c709da1eeae61b6" commit="1b9d1211a9f7226cba7376c67119568f521c750b" commit_message="FROMLIST: dma-buf: Remove range-based flush  [This CL also changes CHROMIUM only omap and pl111 drivers signature]  This patch removes range-based information used for optimizations in begin_cpu_access and end_cpu_access.  We don't have any user nor implementation using range-based flush. It seems a consensus that if we ever want something like that again (or even more robust using 2D, 3D sub-range regions) we can use the upcoming dma-buf sync ioctl for such.  BUG=chromium:487189  Cc: Sumit Semwal &lt;sumit.semwal@linaro.org&gt; Cc: Daniel Vetter &lt;daniel.vetter@intel.com&gt; Signed-off-by: Tiago Vignatti &lt;tiago.vignatti@intel.com&gt;  (cherry-picked from commit a2318821a84a40866105c872263a5c61b47973ee)  Change-Id: I84d5b0c1f41d098bd3c64c7f7c709da1eeae61b6 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/325353 Commit-Ready: Tiago Vignatti &lt;tiago.vignatti@intel.com&gt; Tested-by: Tiago Vignatti &lt;tiago.vignatti@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Stéphane Marchesin &lt;marcheu@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="1" gerrit_number="325353" owner_email="tiago.vignatti@intel.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/53/325353/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="1"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.8" change_id="I287c995bfabc9ba182185e58962ea01c0d489906" commit="5ecfcded09aa0d9bc0c39bc85d4cc576e7eb1411" commit_message="FROMLIST: dma-buf: Add ioctls to allow userspace to flush  The userspace might need some sort of cache coherency management e.g. when CPU and GPU domains are being accessed through dma-buf at the same time. To circumvent this problem there are begin/end coherency markers, that forward directly to existing dma-buf device drivers vfunc hooks. Userspace can make use of those markers through the DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC ioctl. The sequence would be used like following:      - mmap dma-buf fd      - for each drawing/upload cycle in CPU 1. SYNC_START ioctl, 2. read/write        to mmap area 3. SYNC_END ioctl. This can be repeated as often as you        want (with the new data being consumed by the GPU or say scanout device)      - munmap once you don't need the buffer any more  v2 (Tiago): Fix header file type names (u64 -&gt; __u64) v3 (Tiago): Add documentation. Use enum dma_buf_sync_flags to the begin/end dma-buf functions. Check for overflows in start/length. v4 (Tiago): use 2d regions for sync. v5 (Tiago): forget about 2d regions (v4); use _IOW in DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC and remove range information from struct dma_buf_sync. v6 (Tiago): use __u64 structured padded flags instead enum. Adjust documentation about the recommendation on using sync ioctls. v7 (Tiago): Alex' nit on flags definition and being even more wording in the doc about sync usage. v9 (Tiago): remove useless is_dma_buf_file check. Fix sync.flags conditionals and its mask order check. Add &lt;linux/types.h&gt; include in dma-buf.h.  BUG=chromium:487189  Cc: Ville Syrjl &lt;ville.syrjala@linux.intel.com&gt; Cc: David Herrmann &lt;dh.herrmann@gmail.com&gt; Cc: Sumit Semwal &lt;sumit.semwal@linaro.org&gt; Signed-off-by: Daniel Vetter &lt;daniel.vetter@intel.com&gt; Signed-off-by: Tiago Vignatti &lt;tiago.vignatti@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: David Herrmann &lt;dh.herrmann@gmail.com&gt;  Change-Id: I287c995bfabc9ba182185e58962ea01c0d489906 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/325354 Commit-Ready: Tiago Vignatti &lt;tiago.vignatti@intel.com&gt; Tested-by: Tiago Vignatti &lt;tiago.vignatti@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Stéphane Marchesin &lt;marcheu@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="325354" owner_email="tiago.vignatti@intel.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/54/325354/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.8" change_id="Ic819141771cead3253dccef77706488a89e7d8ad" commit="d0a32b8c3ef4f9c533054175877e1faa46ade396" commit_message="FROMLIST: drm/i915: Implement end_cpu_access  This function is meant to be used with dma-buf mmap, when finishing the CPU access of the mapped pointer.  The error case should be rare to happen though, requiring the buffer become active during the sync period and for the end_cpu_access to be interrupted. So we use a uninterruptible mutex_lock to spit out when it ever happens.  v2: disable interruption to make sure errors are reported. v3: update to the new end_cpu_access API. v7: use .write = false cause it doesn't need to know whether it's write.  BUG=chromium:487189  Reviewed-by: Chris Wilson &lt;chris@chris-wilson.co.uk&gt; Signed-off-by: Tiago Vignatti &lt;tiago.vignatti@intel.com&gt; (cherry-picked from commit 261392004f77ebe8e2ea7d31e765a164ce036197)  Change-Id: Ic819141771cead3253dccef77706488a89e7d8ad Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/325355 Commit-Ready: Tiago Vignatti &lt;tiago.vignatti@intel.com&gt; Tested-by: Tiago Vignatti &lt;tiago.vignatti@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Stéphane Marchesin &lt;marcheu@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="325355" owner_email="tiago.vignatti@intel.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/55/325355/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.8" change_id="Ia8d081d7eb3963bc398ea1808e0ce8e9af73eb65" commit="cf7d24af16e7520ea38678fdb67e090d888afaa9" commit_message="FROMLIST: drm/i915: Use CPU mapping for userspace dma-buf mmap()  [REBASED USING UPSTREAM VERSION]  Userspace is the one in charge of flush CPU by wrapping mmap with begin{,end}_cpu_access.  v2: Remove LLC check cause we have dma-buf sync providers now. Also, fix return before transferring ownership when mmap fails. v3: Fix return values. v4: !obj-&gt;base.filp is user triggerable, so removed the WARN_ON.  BUG=chromium:487189  Signed-off-by: Tiago Vignatti &lt;tiago.vignatti@intel.com&gt; (cherry-picked from commit d158696d28e70579a417dc71f584fb2723ceac85)  Change-Id: Ia8d081d7eb3963bc398ea1808e0ce8e9af73eb65 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/325356 Commit-Ready: Tiago Vignatti &lt;tiago.vignatti@intel.com&gt; Tested-by: Tiago Vignatti &lt;tiago.vignatti@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Stéphane Marchesin &lt;marcheu@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="325356" owner_email="tiago.vignatti@intel.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/56/325356/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.14" change_id="Ia4bcc9acb24d004053948690cde9ccba68993c5c" commit="66132fb9d76f47f1b5a1619f5ddc42978ca629d3" commit_message="CHROMIUM: clk: rockchip: Add low jitter 250.5 MHz for better 83.5 MHz HDMI  Same as we've been doing recently.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:50057 TEST=83.5 still works OK and uses new PLL setting.  Change-Id: Ia4bcc9acb24d004053948690cde9ccba68993c5c Signed-off-by: Douglas Anderson &lt;dianders@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/325512 Reviewed-by: Stéphane Marchesin &lt;marcheu@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="3" gerrit_number="325512" owner_email="dianders@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/12/325512/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="3"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.14" change_id="Ic7ce5d2010bf98d192c58e2bc112a6fe1b08ce9c" commit="0bf608dc371d6511bcb697a371d20aeda9d5a14a" commit_message="CHROMIUM: drm: dw_hdmi: Add 83.5 to common_tmds_n_table  This is a pretty normal rate, so add to the main driver. You can see that the &quot;n&quot; in the table can work with an integer CTS to make the exact frequency requested for 44.1kHz.     &gt;&gt;&gt; ((128 * 44100) * 104375) / 7056.    83500000.0  BUG=chrome-os-partner:50057 TEST=83.5 still works OK and uses new PLL setting.  Change-Id: Ic7ce5d2010bf98d192c58e2bc112a6fe1b08ce9c Signed-off-by: Douglas Anderson &lt;dianders@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/325513 Reviewed-by: Stéphane Marchesin &lt;marcheu@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="3" gerrit_number="325513" owner_email="dianders@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/13/325513/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="3"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.14" change_id="I5